const chapters=[
{title:'Scorching',group:'World',tag:'The world',short:'The world before the dragons—and the catastrophe that changed it.',intro:'The Scorching is the name dragons give to the ancient conflict that remade their world. The surviving stories are fragmentary, colored by the viewpoint of the tribe telling them.',sections:[['What is remembered','Long before the present-day dragon kingdoms, humans and dragons fought a devastating war. In dragon accounts, the event is called the Scorching. Humanity’s cities fell, dragons became the dominant civilization, and survivors among humans retreated into hidden or marginal communities.'],['Why it matters','The Scorching is not just a distant battle. It explains the deep fear and contempt many dragons direct toward scavengers, the ruins scattered across Pyrrhia, and the way ancient history can be misread when one people controls the story. The Legends novel Dragonslayer later complicates the familiar account by showing the conflict through human eyes.'],['A changing relationship','The main series gradually challenges the idea that humans are merely pests. Wren learns to communicate with dragons; Sky becomes a bridge between species; and later events force both peoples to confront inherited prejudice.'] ],callout:'Spoiler note: The Scorching’s history is explored most directly in Dragonslayer and in the later main-series books.'},
{title:'MudWings',group:'Pyrrhia',tag:'Pyrrhia',short:'Swamp-country power, close-knit sib groups, and the importance of a warm belly.',intro:'The sturdy MudWings thrive in marshes, rivers, and warm mud. Their culture is organized around siblings: the oldest, the bigwings, is expected to look after the rest.',sections:[['Looks & habitat','MudWings are broad, powerful dragons with earthy brown, amber, or russet scales, lighter underscales, large flat heads, and nostrils high on the snout. Their nostrils help them breathe while submerged. Marshes and wetlands make up much of their kingdom.'],['Abilities','They can stay underwater for a remarkably long time—up to an hour—and can breathe fire when warm. A rare blood-red egg produces a dragonet with fire-resistant scales. Their size and strength are major advantages in a fight.'],['Family and customs','Clutches hatch together and are called sibs. The eldest is the bigwings and traditionally assumes responsibility for the younger dragons. This custom can foster loyalty, but it can also burden a dragon who is still a child. Individual MudWings are often more varied than their reputation for stoicism suggests.'],['Story role','Clay is the central MudWing of the Dragonet Prophecy: gentle, loyal, and stronger than he thinks. Other notable MudWings include Queen Moorhen, Reed, Sora, and Umber.'] ],callout:'A blood-red egg signals fire-resistant scales; warmth also affects whether an ordinary MudWing can produce fire.'},
{title:'SeaWings',group:'Pyrrhia',tag:'Pyrrhia',short:'An underwater kingdom with glowing communication scales and animus history.',intro:'SeaWings are at home beneath the waves. Their ocean kingdom contains palaces, reefs, and deep-water communities, and their culture is shaped by both surface politics and underwater life.',sections:[['Looks & habitat','SeaWings have streamlined bodies, webbed claws, gills, finned tails, and barbels beneath the chin. Their scales can be blue, green, turquoise, or other sea colors. Rows of luminous scales along the body flash in patterns underwater.'],['Abilities and language','They can breathe underwater and swim powerfully. Their bioluminescent scales form Aquatic, a visual language that communicates at depth. SeaWings can also speak above water. The pattern and location of the glow scales help make the language expressive.'],['Royalty and animus magic','The SeaWing royal family has a long and dangerous history with animus magic. Animus dragons can enchant objects, but the power has repeatedly been used to control, punish, or reshape others. Coral’s court, the summer palace, and the Deep Palace feature prominently in the series.'],['Story role','Tsunami is a fierce, decisive Dragonet of Destiny. Anemone and Turtle bring the royal family’s magic and its costs to the foreground; Riptide, Queen Coral, and Fathom are also important.'] ],callout:'Aquatic is made of glowing scale patterns. A dark or unlit room changes what can be communicated.'},
{title:'RainWings',group:'Pyrrhia',tag:'Pyrrhia',short:'Color-changing rainforest dragons with a history of being underestimated.',intro:'RainWings live in a lush rainforest and can change their scale colors to blend into their surroundings—or broadcast exactly how they feel.',sections:[['Looks & habitat','RainWings have long, slender bodies, prehensile tails, and brilliant scales that shift among colors. Their rainforest is rich with fruit, flowers, and canopy paths. The tribe’s society historically placed little emphasis on formal hierarchy.'],['Abilities','Color change provides camouflage and a vivid emotional signal. RainWings can spit a potent venom from their fangs; it rapidly eats through many materials and is dangerous to dragons. They are excellent climbers and use their tails to move through trees. Sunlight and rest matter to their well-being.'],['Culture and change','Before Glory’s reforms, many RainWings had become isolated, politically disengaged, and vulnerable to kidnapping. Glory’s rule reorganizes the tribe, restores defenses, and creates a more active role in international affairs.'],['Story role','Glory overturns other tribes’ assumptions about RainWings and becomes a capable queen. Jambu, Kinkajou, and the former queen Dazzling are among the tribe’s other named characters.'] ],callout:'Color shifts can express mood as well as hide a RainWing. Venom is a serious defense, not a harmless trick.'},
{title:'NightWings',group:'Pyrrhia',tag:'Pyrrhia',short:'A secretive tribe whose moon-born gifts and history shape two arcs.',intro:'NightWings are dark-scaled dragons associated with stars, prophecy, and secrecy. Their reputation grew far beyond what their actual abilities could reliably support.',sections:[['Looks & abilities','NightWings generally have black or very dark scales, silver scale patterns resembling stars, and dark wings. The traditional legends promised mind-reading and foresight to dragonets hatched under the right moons. These gifts depend on the moons at hatching; they are not universal.'],['Volcano exile','For generations the NightWings lived on a volcanic island after leaving the old rainforest home. Their isolation and dwindling resources influenced their politics, propaganda, and conflict with the RainWings. They later resettle in the rainforest.'],['Story role','Starflight is a scholar and Dragonet of Destiny whose knowledge and compassion prove more useful than fighting. Morrowseer, Battlewinner, Deathbringer, Fatespeaker, and Darkstalker reveal very different sides of the tribe.'],['Moon-born talents','Mind-reading receives information from nearby thoughts; foresight offers possible futures rather than a simple guaranteed script. The books emphasize uncertainty, interpretation, and the consequences of treating prophecy as certainty.'] ],callout:'NightWing abilities can fail to appear or be unreliable. The full-moon legend is not a guarantee that every NightWing has powers.'},
{title:'SandWings',group:'Pyrrhia',tag:'Pyrrhia',short:'Desert survival, a venomous tail barb, and a succession war.',intro:'SandWings occupy the great desert of Pyrrhia. Their kingdom’s history is inseparable from the succession crisis after Queen Oasis’s death.',sections:[['Looks & abilities','SandWings are pale gold, cream, or tan, with long necks and tails ending in a venomous barb. They can withstand desert heat and have a fire-resistant body compared with many tribes. Their tail barb is a formidable weapon.'],['The three sisters','Burn, Blister, and Blaze each gathered allies after Oasis died. Their rivalry divided Pyrrhia and became the central crisis of the first arc. The sisters embody different kinds of power: brute force, political manipulation, and charm backed by other dragons’ support.'],['A kingdom beyond the dunes','Oases, strongholds, and trade routes make survival possible. Scavenger settlements and ancient human ruins also dot the desert, though dragons’ interpretation of those sites is often incomplete.'],['Story role','Qibli’s wit and curiosity bring a fresh perspective to SandWing politics. Thorn and her Outclaws offer a different model of leadership; Sunny, a Dragonet of Destiny, is half SandWing.'] ],callout:'The barb is venomous. Do not confuse it with the stinger-like tail designs of other fictional dragons.'},
{title:'IceWings',group:'Pyrrhia',tag:'Pyrrhia',short:'A frigid northern realm, strict status customs, and freezing breath.',intro:'IceWings are adapted to an unforgiving arctic kingdom. Their history includes a rigid ranking system and old animus magic.',sections:[['Looks & abilities','IceWings have pale scales—often white, silver, or icy blue—along with horns, ridged scales, and sharp claws suited to gripping ice. Frostbreath can freeze a target. They tolerate cold and bright snow glare better than other tribes.'],['The IceWing wall','A social ranking system historically sorted IceWings into circles with differing privileges. It shaped where dragons could live, whom they could marry, and what opportunities they received. Winter’s story exposes its costs and the possibility of change.'],['History and magic','The IceWings once had animus dragons. Arctic’s departure to the NightWings and the enchantments surrounding Darkstalker are part of the chain of events that links the tribes’ histories. The old gift was feared and eventually ceased to be a common part of IceWing life.'],['Story role','Winter begins as proud and defensive but learns to question his tribe’s rules. Queen Glacier, Hailstorm, Snowfall, and Arctic are key figures in different eras.'] ],callout:'Frostbreath is a freezing weapon. IceWing society’s ranking traditions are cultural rules, not biological traits.'},
{title:'SkyWings',group:'Pyrrhia',tag:'Pyrrhia',short:'Fast fliers from mountain eyries, with fire and a complicated royal past.',intro:'SkyWings are associated with high peaks, speed, and powerful flight. Their kingdom was drawn into the SandWing succession war under Queen Scarlet.',sections:[['Looks & abilities','SkyWings tend toward warm red, orange, and gold scales, though individuals vary. Their wings are large and their flight is renowned. Fire is a characteristic defense. Firescales dragons generate dangerous heat through touch, while some SkyWings are born with unusually little or no fire.'],['Culture and history','Mountain territory and aerial strength are central to SkyWing life. Scarlet’s arena and court turn dragon combat into spectacle and political control. Her rule leaves lasting scars even after she loses power.'],['Story role','Peril is a firescales dragon who has been treated as a weapon; her arc is about choice, identity, and being valued beyond her power. Sky, Ruby, Scarlet, and Cliff are other significant SkyWings.'],['Firescales','A firescales dragon’s body burns intensely enough to injure those nearby. A cooling earring made by an animus provides Peril a way to touch others more safely, while also raising questions about who controls her body.'] ],callout:'Peril’s heat is a rare condition, not the normal SkyWing experience.'},
{title:'Legend of the Hive',group:'Pantala',tag:'Pantala',short:'The origin story of Pantala’s tribes, told through a contested legend.',intro:'On Pantala, a story about a great queen, a gifted tribe, and a division between dragons becomes the foundation for a political order.',sections:[['The legend','The HiveWing account remembers Queen Wasp as the unifier of Pantala and celebrates the HiveWings’ power. The history behind this public story is far darker and more complicated. Clearsight’s arrival, the BeetleWings, and the division into new tribes are central to the real past.'],['Three tribes, one past','SilkWings and HiveWings descend from BeetleWings, with NightWing ancestry entering the lineage through Clearsight’s descendants. LeafWings remain connected to Pantala’s forests and to the catastrophe that reshaped the continent.'],['Control of memory','The series asks who gets to write history. HiveWing schools and government use a simplified narrative to justify power, while hidden records and living witnesses reveal erased experiences.'],['Where to read more','The Lost Continent begins the Pantala arc. The Poison Jungle and The Dangerous Gift widen the conflict; The Guide to the Dragon World and Legends: Darkstalker add earlier context.'] ],callout:'The official HiveWing version is propaganda inside the story. Treat it as a character viewpoint, not neutral narration.'},
{title:'SilkWings',group:'Pantala',tag:'Pantala',short:'Butterfly-like dragons, silk threads, and rare flamesilk.',intro:'SilkWings are graceful Pantalan dragons whose metamorphosis and silk production once made them vulnerable to HiveWing control.',sections:[['Looks & life cycle','SilkWings have two pairs of wings, antennae, and often colorful scales. Young dragonets begin with wingbuds and undergo metamorphosis into adults. Their appearance varies widely.'],['Silk and flamesilk','SilkWings can produce silk from glands in their wrists. A rare trait called flamesilk lets a dragon produce burning threads. The HiveWing state exploited flamesilk SilkWings and controlled their lives through forced service.'],['Society under Wasp','Under Queen Wasp’s rule, SilkWings were compelled to obey HiveWings and sorted into social roles. The Book of Clearsight and the Chrysalis resistance preserve forbidden knowledge and organize opposition.'],['Story role','Blue is thoughtful and gentle; Luna is bold and determined. Cricket’s questions help expose the control systems embedded in ordinary Pantalan life. Swordtail, Io, and Admiral are additional major SilkWings.'] ],callout:'Metamorphosis is a life stage in canon. Flamesilk is rare, and not every SilkWing can produce it.'},
{title:'HiveWings',group:'Pantala',tag:'Pantala',short:'A powerful Pantalan tribe shaped by venom, hierarchy, and Wasp’s control.',intro:'HiveWings are a diverse tribe living in enormous hive structures. Their government’s apparent unity conceals coercion and resistance.',sections:[['Looks & abilities','HiveWings have two wings, antennae, and a wide range of colors and patterns. Individual abilities can include stingers, venom, strong jaws, or chemical defenses, but no single power should be assumed of every dragon.'],['Hives and government','HiveWings live in named Hives, each a densely organized community. Queen Wasp’s control over the tribe is reinforced by political institutions and a supernatural connection to the Breath of Evil. HiveWing obedience is not the same as freely chosen loyalty.'],['Resistance','The Chrysalis network resists the regime and helps SilkWings escape control. HiveWing characters make clear that people inside a harmful system can question it, collaborate, or be coerced.'],['Story role','Cricket is curious and analytically minded, and becomes pivotal in uncovering the regime’s secrets. Sundew’s family history, Lady Scarab, Swordtail, and Wasp herself anchor the Pantala conflict.'] ],callout:'Abilities vary between HiveWings; avoid reading any one character’s trait as universal.'},
{title:'LeafWings',group:'Pantala',tag:'Pantala',short:'Forest guardians with leafspeak, divided by how to survive loss.',intro:'LeafWings are tied to Pantala’s forests and the struggle to preserve them after the Burning of the Poison Jungle.',sections:[['Looks & abilities','LeafWings have leaf-shaped wings and green or earthy coloring that helps them blend into forest environments. Some are born with leafspeak, an ability to communicate with and influence plants. Its strength varies.'],['Two paths','After their homeland was devastated, LeafWings split into groups with opposing strategies. The SapWings prioritize rebuilding and coexistence; the PoisonWings favor fighting back against those they blame. Both positions arise from grief and fear.'],['The Poison Jungle','The jungle is dangerous to dragons and extraordinarily rich in unusual plants. LeafWing knowledge makes it possible to navigate and understand an ecosystem that outsiders underestimate.'],['Story role','Sundew is a fierce PoisonWing who must decide what justice means. Willow, Hazel, and Belladonna show contrasting ways to care for the tribe and its future.'] ],callout:'Leafspeak is a rare gift. It is not an ability shared by every LeafWing.'},
{title:'BeetleWings (Extinct)',group:'Pantala',tag:'Pantala',short:'The ancestral Pantalan tribe remembered through ruins, descendants, and fragments.',intro:'BeetleWings are the ancestral tribe from which modern SilkWings and HiveWings descend. They are considered extinct as a distinct people in the present-day story.',sections:[['What the books establish','BeetleWings lived on ancient Pantala before the modern tribes formed. Their descendants became SilkWings and HiveWings after the arrival of Clearsight and the rise of new political divisions.'],['What remains uncertain','The series gives glimpses and records rather than a full ethnography of BeetleWing life. Specific looks and customs are not described with the same detail as the living tribes. Some “BeetleWing” art online is fan interpretation.'],['Why they matter','The lost tribe is a reminder that present-day labels are not timeless. Pantala’s history contains migration, intermarriage, political change, and deliberate erasure.'],['Research trail','Explore Legends: Darkstalker for Clearsight’s era and the Pantala books for the descendants’ understanding of their origins. The companion Guide to the Dragon World adds worldbuilding.'] ],callout:'Extinct here means no longer present as a distinct living tribe in the narrative; the people survive through descendants.'},
{title:'Humans',group:'World',tag:'The world',short:'The other intelligent species—and the series’ changing view of scavengers.',intro:'Humans are called scavengers by dragons, a name that reflects dragon-centric assumptions. The books gradually reveal humans as people with their own language, history, families, and choices.',sections:[['Scavengers in dragon society','Most dragons initially regard humans as small animals and hunt them. This belief is reinforced by the aftermath of the Scorching and by generations of limited contact. Dragon characters who question it face ridicule or danger.'],['Human perspectives','Dragonslayer follows Wren, Leaf, and Ivy during the succession war and gives humans the narrative center. Wren learns dragon speech and communicates with Sky, making cross-species understanding possible.'],['A shifting balance','As the series progresses, humans become participants in events rather than objects at the edge of the page. Their knowledge of ruins and ability to interact with dragons can alter political outcomes.'],['Key humans','Wren is a brave communicator; Leaf is driven by his family and the dragon he believes killed them; Ivy is an observant survivor and planner. Other humans and scavenger communities appear across the books.'] ],callout:'“Scavenger” is the dragons’ term. It reflects their worldview, not an objective description of humans.'},
{title:'Animus Power',group:'Magic',tag:'Magic & lore',short:'Enchantments that can transform the world—and the user’s sense of self.',intro:'Animus magic lets a dragon enchant objects and, in some cases, living beings. The books treat the power as morally dangerous because choices and institutions shape its use.',sections:[['How it works','An animus can give an object a magical instruction, often by speaking or writing an enchantment. The precise limits are not always clear, and enchanted objects can persist after their maker dies. Commands may be literal, broad, or dangerously ambiguous.'],['The soul question','A longstanding belief says every use of animus magic damages the user’s soul. The story questions how much of this is a fact of magic and how much is fear, social teaching, or the pressure of unchecked power. Cruelty is a choice; magic does not automatically make a dragon evil.'],['Known animus dragons','Notable animus dragons include Albatross, Fathom, Turtle, Anemone, Arctic, Darkstalker, and Jerboa. Their experiences show that power can be inherited, hidden, feared, or used to protect.'],['Consequences and consent','Enchantment can override another dragon’s body, memory, or agency. The most consequential moral questions are who gave consent, who bears the risk, and whether the enchantment can be reversed.'],['Related abilities','Animus magic is different from NightWing mind-reading and foresight, RainWing venom, firescales, flamesilk, and leafspeak. Those gifts have distinct origins and limits.'] ],callout:'The novels do not provide a neat, consistent rulebook for every enchantment. Read each spell in context and watch for unintended wording.'},
{title:'Dragon Biology & Culture',group:'Reference',tag:'Field notes',short:'A quick cross-tribe reference to wings, senses, speech, and social life.',intro:'Wings of Fire’s dragons are intelligent, language-using animals with distinct anatomy and cultures. Their traits differ by tribe, while individuals still vary.',sections:[['Anatomy','Most adult dragons have four limbs and two wings. Tribes adapt to their habitats: SeaWings have gills and webbing; MudWings have strong builds and high-set nostrils; IceWings have frostbreath; and Pantalan dragon lineages have distinct wing forms.'],['Communication','Dragons speak aloud. SeaWings additionally use Aquatic through glowing scales. Body posture, facial expression, scent, and color can also convey information. Dragon speech and human speech are not automatically mutually intelligible.'],['Life and society','Queens usually lead tribes, but leadership systems vary and change. Families, sibling groups, royal courts, soldiers, scholars, and resistance groups all shape dragon life. The books show traditions being questioned and rebuilt.'],['Hybrids and identity','Dragonets can inherit traits from more than one tribe. A hybrid’s appearance and abilities may be unpredictable; ancestry can affect how others treat them, but it does not dictate their loyalties or personality.'] ],callout:'The books’ field-guide pages vary in detail. This section summarizes recurring canon rather than inventing a universal biology manual.'},
{title:'Continents & Places',group:'Reference',tag:'Geography',short:'Map the kingdoms, borders, landmarks, and dangerous wild places.',intro:'The series centers on Pyrrhia and Pantala, two continents with distinct climates and political histories. Maps and borders shift as characters travel and governments change.',sections:[['Pyrrhia','The seven Pyrrhian kingdoms include the Mud Kingdom’s marshes, the Sea Kingdom’s underwater palaces, the Rainforest Kingdom, the NightWing volcano and later rainforest home, the Sand Kingdom and Scorpion Den, the Ice Kingdom in the north, and the Sky Kingdom’s mountains and arenas.'],['Pantala','Pantala contains the Hives, SilkWing towns, and the forests and poisonous ecosystem of the Poison Jungle. The Hives are political and population centers; the jungle is both refuge and hazard.'],['Jade Mountain','The academy is built into a mountain in Pyrrhia and becomes neutral ground where young dragons from different tribes study together. Its caves and tunnels also expose students to old secrets and threats.'],['Travel and borders','Flight makes distance meaningful but does not erase terrain, weather, or political barriers. Sea routes, mountain passes, desert water, and unfamiliar forests all shape who can travel safely.'] ],callout:'Place names and borders are easiest to follow with the maps printed in the books and companion guide.'},
{title:'Books & Reading Order',group:'Reference',tag:'Reading guide',short:'A spoiler-aware map of the arcs, Legends, Winglets, and companion guides.',intro:'The main series is arranged in three five-book arcs. Side stories revisit earlier periods or add a different point of view, so placement depends on whether you prefer publication order or chronological context.',sections:[['Arc One · The Dragonet Prophecy','Books 1–5: The Dragonet Prophecy, The Lost Heir, The Hidden Kingdom, The Dark Secret, and The Brightest Night. These follow the five dragonets and the SandWing succession war.'],['Arc Two · The Jade Mountain Prophecy','Books 6–10: Moon Rising, Winter Turning, Escaping Peril, Talons of Power, and Darkness of Dragons. The academy brings the next generation together as Darkstalker returns.'],['Arc Three · The Lost Continent Prophecy','Books 11–15: The Lost Continent, The Hive Queen, The Poison Jungle, The Dangerous Gift, and The Flames of Hope. The focus shifts to Pantala, Wasp’s rule, and the conflict across continents.'],['Legends and side stories','Legends: Darkstalker is set long before the main series; Dragonslayer follows humans during the first arc’s war. The Winglets—Prisoners, Assassin, Deserter, and Runaway—follow supporting characters. A Guide to the Dragon World is an illustrated companion, not a numbered novel.'],['A good first read','Reading books 1–15 in order preserves character reveals. Then try the Legends, Winglets, and companion guide for expanded context. Dragonslayer contains human perspectives and first-arc spoilers.'] ],callout:'WOFFG discusses spoilers for the full main series and companion stories.'},
{title:'War, Prophecy & Power',group:'History',tag:'History',short:'How prophecies, armies, and succession claims steer—and fail to control—events.',intro:'Wings of Fire repeatedly tests the stories leaders tell to justify power. Prophecy matters, but the characters’ decisions matter more.',sections:[['Talons of Peace','The Talons of Peace is a secret organization formed to end the SandWing war. They hide and raise dragonets as the supposed answer to a prophecy, but their plans involve kidnapping, secrecy, and risk.'],['Prophecy as politics','Morrowseer’s prophecy is fabricated to manipulate events. The five dragonets nevertheless become central to ending the war through choices nobody could fully script. Later arcs question whether seeing possible futures gives anyone the right to control others.'],['Succession and legitimacy','The first war grows from a disputed succession. Later leadership changes—Glory’s reforms, Thorn’s rule, Ruby’s succession, and Snowfall’s responsibilities—show different approaches to legitimacy and public trust.'],['War’s cost','The series depicts injury, grief, displacement, captivity, trauma, and inherited resentment. Characters such as Sora, Peril, Winter, and Sundew carry consequences long after a battle ends.'] ],callout:'A prophecy can shape behavior even when its source is false. In this story, belief itself has political power.'},
{title:'Organizations & Resistance',group:'Reference',tag:'Factions',short:'The groups that protect, rule, teach, or resist across both continents.',intro:'The dragons’ world is shaped by organizations as much as by tribes. Some begin with good intentions and cause harm; others are born as resistance to oppressive power.',sections:[['Talons of Peace','A Pyrrhian secret society tries to end the succession war by raising the Dragonets of Destiny. Its secret methods become part of the dragonets’ conflict with the prophecy.'],['Jade Mountain Academy','Founded by Clay, Tsunami, Glory, Starflight, and Sunny, the school gathers dragonets from rival tribes. It is both an educational project and a fragile experiment in peace.'],['The Outclaws','Thorn’s group operates in the Sand Kingdom and offers protection and structure outside the old royal order. Its members become politically important after the succession crisis.'],['The Chrysalis','On Pantala, the Chrysalis resistance opposes Queen Wasp and helps SilkWings and other targets of the regime. Secret networks, coded communication, and trust are essential to survival.'],['The SapWings and PoisonWings','LeafWing groups respond differently to the destruction of their home. Their conflict is rooted in grief, fear, and competing ideas about safety and justice.'] ],callout:'Organizations contain individuals with different motives. A faction label does not tell you everything about a character.'},
{title:'Glossary & Powers',group:'Reference',tag:'Glossary',short:'Quick definitions for recurring terms, abilities, and story concepts.',intro:'A compact field glossary for terms that recur throughout the books. Some labels are used by dragons in-world and carry their biases.',sections:[['Aquatic','The SeaWing visual language made with bioluminescent scales.'],['Animus','A dragon with enchantment magic. “Animus” is a magical ability and social identity, not a tribe.'],['Bigwings','The eldest MudWing sib, traditionally expected to care for younger siblings.'],['Dragonet','A young dragon. The books use age and life-stage terms in ways that depend on tribe and context.'],['Firescales','A rare SkyWing condition in which a dragon’s body generates dangerous heat.'],['Flamesilk','Silk produced by a rare SilkWing ability; it burns as it is spun.'],['Leafspeak','A rare LeafWing gift involving communication with and influence over plants.'],['Scavenger','The dragons’ in-world term for humans, reflecting their initial failure to recognize humans as people.'],['Sib','A MudWing sibling from the same clutch.'] ],callout:'For full explanations and limits, open the matching tribe or Animus Power chapter.'}
];
const dragons=[
['Clay','MudWing','Dragonet of Destiny; healer-hearted hero','Large, earthy-scaled and powerfully built, with the sturdy silhouette typical of his tribe.','Gentle, loyal, food-loving, and protective; initially doubts his own courage.','One of five dragonets raised to fulfill the prophecy; repeatedly chooses compassion over violence.'],
['Tsunami','SeaWing','Dragonet of Destiny; princess and leader','Blue-green SeaWing with glowing scales, webbed claws, and a strong swimmer’s build.','Bold, impatient, decisive, and fiercely protective of friends.','Her search for her family reveals the SeaWing royal court and the dangers around the succession war.'],
['Glory','RainWing / NightWing','Dragonet of Destiny; queen','Color-changing RainWing with vivid scales; her NightWing heritage is part of the first arc’s revelations.','Sharp-witted, skeptical, brave, and unwilling to accept low expectations.','Helps end the war and reforms the RainWing tribe before becoming queen of RainWings and NightWings.'],
['Starflight','NightWing','Dragonet of Destiny; scholar','Dark NightWing with silver star-like markings and large wings.','Bookish, cautious, thoughtful, and deeply caring beneath his anxiety.','His knowledge and moral courage become crucial even when prophecy-gifts and fighting are absent.'],
['Sunny','SandWing hybrid','Dragonet of Destiny','Small, bright-gold dragon with rounded features; her hybrid ancestry was hidden from her.','Optimistic, curious, kind, and quietly persistent.','Challenges fatalistic readings of the prophecy and helps bring the first arc to a more hopeful resolution.'],
['Queen Thorn','SandWing','Outclaw leader; queen','Pale desert-colored SandWing with the tribe’s characteristic venomous tail barb.','Practical, protective, tough-minded, and more compassionate than her reputation suggests.','Builds the Outclaws and becomes a stabilizing SandWing ruler after the succession conflict.'],
['Qibli','SandWing','Scholar-minded strategist; Jade Mountain student','Gold and brown SandWing with a long, barbed tail.','Funny, observant, socially perceptive, and hungry to prove his worth.','A central viewpoint character in arc two; his choices test the appeal and limits of power.'],
['Winter','IceWing','Prince; Jade Mountain student','Pale IceWing with icy scales, horns, and the sharp claws of his tribe.','Proud, prickly, principled, and capable of deep loyalty and growth.','Confronts the IceWing ranking system and learns that care can require challenging his family.'],
['Peril','SkyWing','Firescales dragon; arena champion','Bright orange-red SkyWing whose intense heat makes ordinary touch dangerous.','Impulsive, sincere, lonely, and eager to be valued for more than her fire.','Escapes Queen Scarlet’s weaponized arena and becomes a key ally across multiple arcs.'],
['Turtle','SeaWing','Animus; Jade Mountain student','Green-blue SeaWing with luminous scales and an unobtrusive manner.','Gentle, nervous, imaginative, and more courageous than he believes.','His animus magic and relationship with Anemone put consent and fear at the heart of arc two.'],
['Kinkajou','RainWing','Jade Mountain student','Small RainWing whose scales can flash through bright, playful colors.','Energetic, affectionate, impulsive, and steadfast under pressure.','Becomes a close friend to the academy dragonets and helps resist Darkstalker.'],
['Moonwatcher','NightWing','Mind-reader; Jade Mountain student','Dark NightWing with the starry markings and eyes associated with her tribe.','Empathetic, anxious, perceptive, and burdened by thoughts she never asked to hear.','Her mind-reading and visions begin arc two and expose the continuing threat of Darkstalker.'],
['Winter','IceWing','Prince','A lean pale dragon with frosty scales and prominent IceWing horns.','See also his central arc-two role: defensive pride softens into empathy.','Jade Mountain protagonist; duplicates omitted in the directory.'],
['Darkstalker','NightWing / IceWing','Ancient animus; antagonist of arc two','Enormous dark dragon with NightWing stars and inherited IceWing features.','Brilliant, charismatic, controlling, and convinced he knows what is best.','His ancient rise and return drive Legends: Darkstalker and the second arc.'],
['Fathom','SeaWing','Animus; Darkstalker-era prince','Blue SeaWing with glowing scales and royal features.','Sensitive, thoughtful, and morally troubled by the power he inherited.','His friendship with Darkstalker and role in Albatross’s tragedy define the ancient history.'],
['Albatross','SeaWing','Animus; royal court architect','Large SeaWing with pale, luminous scales and an imposing presence.','Initially valued for skill; increasingly resentful and dangerous under pressure.','His violence at the SeaWing palace becomes a foundational trauma in SeaWing history.'],
['Arctic','IceWing','Animus; Darkstalker’s father','Pale IceWing with dark markings from his NightWing life and ancestry.','Proud, fearful, and shaped by isolation and a disastrous family conflict.','His flight from the Ice Kingdom sets the chain of events that leads to Darkstalker.'],
['Clearsight','NightWing','Prophet; Pantala ancestor','Dark NightWing with silver scales and eyes; depictions emphasize her striking presence.','Compassionate, intelligent, cautious, and overwhelmed by possible futures.','Leaves Pyrrhia for Pantala and becomes an ancestor to its modern dragon tribes.'],
['Morrowseer','NightWing','Prophet; architect of the prophecy','Dark NightWing, imposing and marked by silver patterns.','Authoritative, manipulative, and willing to use belief for political ends.','Creates the false prophecy that shapes the Dragonets of Destiny’s childhood.'],
['Queen Coral','SeaWing','Queen; mother of Tsunami and Anemone','Royal SeaWing with a commanding presence and luminous aquatic markings.','Grieving, controlling, protective, and prone to letting fear rule her court.','Her rule and family decisions frame the SeaWing palace conflicts of arc one.'],
['Anemone','SeaWing','Princess; animus dragon','Young royal SeaWing with bright scales and glowing marks.','Privileged, volatile, lonely, and struggling to define herself outside others’ plans.','Her animus power and relationship with Turtle become central to arc two’s magic dilemma.'],
['Riptide','SeaWing','Guard and Tsunami’s ally','Blue SeaWing adapted to the sea, with webbed claws and luminous markings.','Reserved, dutiful, and quietly brave.','Helps Tsunami navigate SeaWing politics and later supports the protagonists.'],
['Blister','SandWing','Princess; succession claimant','Elegant pale SandWing with the tribe’s barbed tail.','Calculating, persuasive, and ruthless in pursuit of the throne.','A principal antagonist in the first arc’s succession war.'],
['Burn','SandWing','Princess; succession claimant','Massive, scarred SandWing built for intimidation and combat.','Violent, blunt, and convinced strength should decide rule.','Her military following drives much of the war’s battlefield action.'],
['Blaze','SandWing','Princess; succession claimant','Glamorous pale-gold SandWing with carefully maintained appearance.','Vain, friendly, and politically naïve compared with her sisters.','Her claim draws IceWing support and helps keep the succession conflict alive.'],
['Queen Scarlet','SkyWing','Former queen; arena ruler','Red SkyWing with a regal, battle-ready look.','Cruel, theatrical, and obsessed with spectacle and control.','Captures Peril and turns the SkyWing arena into a tool of terror.'],
['Queen Ruby','SkyWing','Queen; Scarlet’s daughter','Red-orange SkyWing with bright scales and a warm royal bearing.','Fair-minded and patient, though marked by the danger of her family history.','Replaces Scarlet and helps stabilize SkyWing rule.'],
['Deathbringer','NightWing','Assassin; later ally','Dark NightWing with silver marks and a sleek, agile build.','Sarcastic, skilled, guarded, and surprisingly loyal.','Begins as an assassin sent against Glory; later becomes an important ally and partner.'],
['Fatespeaker','NightWing','False-prophecy dragonet','Dark NightWing with starry scales and a gentle, open expression.','Hopeful, empathetic, and earnest about helping others.','Her belief in the prophecy helps expose the difference between Morrowseer’s scheme and the dragonets’ real choices.'],
['Queen Moorhen','MudWing','Queen','Large, brown-toned MudWing with strong, armored proportions.','Steady, diplomatic, and measured.','Leads the MudWings through the succession war and its aftermath.'],
['Sora','MudWing','Dragonet of the academy; former soldier','Broad, earth-colored MudWing with a strong build.','Quiet, grieving, wary, and protective of her brother.','Her choices reveal the long human cost of the war among the dragonets themselves.'],
['Umber','MudWing','Dragonet; Sora’s brother','Young, solidly built MudWing with earthy scales.','Kind, friendly, and committed to finding a peaceful path.','His disappearance from Sora’s life intensifies her guilt and shapes her story.'],
['Dazzling','RainWing','Former queen','Color-changing RainWing whose exact hues shift with mood and surroundings.','Relaxed and disengaged in the period before Glory’s reforms.','Her reign illustrates how the RainWing tribe’s old political neglect left it vulnerable.'],
['Sunny','SandWing hybrid','Dragonet of Destiny','Golden hybrid with a small frame and bright coloring.','Optimistic and brave; one of the first arc’s moral centers.','See above; included once in the directory.'],
['Wasp','HiveWing','Queen of Pantala; antagonist','A large HiveWing queen with vivid coloring and a commanding silhouette.','Authoritarian, strategic, and adept at turning fear into obedience.','Uses the Breath of Evil to control HiveWings and sustain her rule over Pantala.'],
['Cricket','HiveWing','Scholar; Pantala protagonist','Yellow-and-black HiveWing with antennae and the tribe’s two-winged form.','Curious, candid, brave, and relentlessly analytical.','Her questions uncover the regime’s hidden history and help drive the third arc.'],
['Blue','SilkWing','Pantala protagonist','Blue-scaled SilkWing with antennae and four wings as an adult.','Gentle, conscientious, and more resilient than he first appears.','His family is caught in Wasp’s system; he becomes an active participant in resistance.'],
['Luna','SilkWing','Flamesilk; Pantala protagonist','SilkWing with colorful scales and the striking wings of her tribe.','Bold, independent, impatient with injustice, and deeply loyal.','Her flamesilk makes her a target; her journey brings Pyrrhia and Pantala together.'],
['Sundew','LeafWing','PoisonWing; resistance fighter','Green LeafWing with leaf-like wings and camouflage coloring.','Fierce, outspoken, strategic, and protective of her people.','Her story examines revenge, family trauma, and the meaning of justice in arc three.'],
['Willow','LeafWing','SapWing; Sundew’s partner','LeafWing with green coloring and foliage-shaped wings.','Patient, observant, compassionate, and quietly resilient.','Represents a rebuilding-focused response to Pantala’s destruction.'],
['Hazel','LeafWing','SapWing leader','LeafWing whose green, leaflike form reflects her forest home.','Thoughtful, brave, and committed to negotiating survival.','Works to unite LeafWings and protect what remains of their forest.'],
['Belladonna','LeafWing','PoisonWing leader','Green LeafWing with the tribe’s distinctive leaf-shaped wings.','Hard-edged and vengeful after loss.','Leads the most militant response to the Burning and challenges Sundew’s loyalties.'],
['Queen Snowfall','IceWing','Queen; protagonist of The Dangerous Gift','Pale IceWing with crisp features and an imposing royal appearance.','Anxious, defensive, sharp-tongued, and capable of growth.','Her novella-length story tests her isolationist instincts and responsibility as queen.'],
['Wren','Human','Human protagonist; dragon speaker','A human girl with practical survival gear and the small stature dragons underestimate.','Clever, stubborn, courageous, and curious about dragons.','Learns dragon speech and forms a friendship with Sky, opening a new human-dragon perspective.'],
['Sky','SkyWing','Dragon protagonist of Dragonslayer','A young SkyWing with a warm red-orange appearance.','Gentle, trusting, and fascinated by the world beyond his cave.','Wren’s dragon companion; his life contrasts with Peril’s and reveals SkyWing history from below.'],
['Ivy','Human','Human protagonist of Dragonslayer','A resourceful human girl shaped by life around the stronghold.','Practical, perceptive, and brave in a crisis.','Shows how humans organize and survive while dragon kingdoms wage war.'],
['Leaf','Human','Human protagonist of Dragonslayer','A young human carrying the anger and grief of his family’s loss.','Determined, protective, and initially driven by revenge.','His search for answers intersects with the SandWing succession conflict.'],
['Jerboa','SandWing','Animus; generations of spellcasters','A SandWing whose appearance and age are complicated by generations of enchanted bodies.','Guarded, exhausted, and desperate to escape inherited expectations.','Her spells and legacy form a key part of the third arc’s conflict over animus magic.'],
['Queen Glacier','IceWing','Queen','Pale IceWing queen, elegant and adapted to the northern cold.','Regal, pragmatic, and protective of IceWing tradition.','Her death and political legacy shape Winter’s family and the IceWing kingdom.'],
['Hailstorm','IceWing','Prince; prisoner of Scarlet','Pale IceWing with the characteristic sharp claws and frosty scales.','Honorable, kind, and disoriented by what was done to him.','His captivity and return reveal the damage caused by Scarlet and the IceWing court.'],
['Cliff','SkyWing','Young prince','A young red-orange SkyWing with developing wings.','Playful, curious, and spirited.','Represents the next generation of SkyWings after Scarlet’s rule.'],
['Cobra','SandWing','Outclaw; Qibli’s mother','SandWing with desert coloring and a barbed tail.','Cunning, controlling, and shaped by a life of survival.','Her abuse and manipulation help explain Qibli’s past without defining his future.'],
['Vulture','SandWing','Crime lord; Thorn’s rival','Older SandWing with a hardened, imposing presence.','Ambitious, calculating, and power hungry.','A continuing threat to Thorn’s authority and SandWing stability.'],
['Tamarin','RainWing','RainWing student','RainWing with camouflage scales and physical disabilities affecting her sight.','Patient, perceptive, and self-assured.','Her presence at Jade Mountain expands the series’ portrayal of disability and access.'],
['Jambu','RainWing','RainWing student; Glory’s brother','Bright, color-shifting RainWing with a relaxed rainforest style.','Easygoing, affectionate, and supportive.','Shows the ordinary family life that Glory tries to protect and reform.'],
['Mightyclaws','NightWing','Jade Mountain student','Dark NightWing with star-marked scales.','Earnest, excitable, and fascinated by stories.','One of the younger academy dragons who help make the school a community.'],
['Bumblebee','SilkWing / HiveWing lineage','Young dragonet; third-arc companion','Tiny bright-colored dragonet with traits of the Pantalan tribes.','Loud, curious, and affectionate.','A symbol of the next generation and the relationships formed across tribal lines.'],
['Admiral','SilkWing','SilkWing community leader','Adult SilkWing with antennae and the characteristic paired wings.','Protective, careful, and shaped by surviving political repression.','Blue’s father; his choices show how families navigate Wasp’s rule.']
];
const timeline=[['Before the main series','Ancient dragon history','The Scorching reshapes the relationship between dragons and humans. The BeetleWings and LeafWings settle Pantala; Clearsight eventually arrives, and Pantala’s later tribal history begins.'],['About 2,000 years before the present','Darkstalker’s era','Darkstalker, Clearsight, Fathom, Arctic, and Albatross shape an era of animus magic, prophecy, and political fracture. Darkstalker is enchanted into enchanted sleep beneath the mountain.'],['A generation before the prophecy','The SandWing succession crisis begins','Queen Oasis dies. Her daughters Burn, Blister, and Blaze gather allies, drawing tribes into a long civil war. The Talons of Peace hide five dragonets meant to end it.'],['The Dragonet Prophecy · books 1–5','War and a new RainWing queen','Clay, Tsunami, Glory, Starflight, and Sunny discover that prophecy is not a substitute for choice. The succession war ends; Glory becomes queen and the NightWings leave the volcanic island.'],['The Jade Mountain Prophecy · books 6–10','A school and an ancient return','The academy brings dragonets from rival tribes together. Moonwatcher’s mind-reading reveals Darkstalker’s return, and the students must confront his enchantments and influence.'],['The Lost Continent Prophecy · books 11–15','Pantala’s regime challenged','Blue, Cricket, Sundew, and their allies expose Queen Wasp’s control, the Breath of Evil, and the erased history of their continent. The crisis binds the continents together.'],['Across the arcs','Humans enter the conversation','Dragonslayer and later events give humans distinct voices, alliances, and stakes. The assumed divide between dragons and “scavengers” begins to break down.'],['Companion stories','History from other viewpoints','Legends: Darkstalker and Dragonslayer, the Winglets, and A Guide to the Dragon World add perspectives on older events, side characters, and tribal life.']];
const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
function tribeOf(entry){const tribes=['MudWings','SeaWings','RainWings','NightWings','SandWings','IceWings','SkyWings','SilkWings','HiveWings','LeafWings','BeetleWings'];const matches=(entry.categories||[]).filter(c=>tribes.includes(c));return matches.length?matches.join(' / '):(entry.categories||[]).includes('Hybrids')?'Hybrid':'Unknown / multiple'}
function init(){const grid=document.querySelector('#chapterGrid');grid.innerHTML=chapters.map((c,i)=>`<button class="chapter-card" data-chapter="${i}"><span class="num">${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')} · ${esc(c.group)}</span><span class="arrow">↗</span><h3>${esc(c.title)}</h3><p>${esc(c.short)}</p></button>`).join('');grid.addEventListener('click',e=>{const b=e.target.closest('[data-chapter]');if(b)showChapter(+b.dataset.chapter)});
const tribes=[...new Set((window.dragonCatalog||[]).map(tribeOf))].sort();document.querySelector('#tribeFilter').insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend',tribes.map(t=>`<option>${esc(t)}</option>`).join(''));
document.querySelector('#timelineList').innerHTML=timeline.map(e=>`<article class="event"><time>${esc(e[0])} · ${esc(e[1])}</time><p>${esc(e[2])}</p></article>`).join('');renderDragons();document.querySelector('#search').addEventListener('input',()=>{dragonPage=0;renderDragons()});document.querySelector('#tribeFilter').addEventListener('change',()=>{dragonPage=0;renderDragons()});document.querySelector('#backButton').onclick=()=>location.hash='#guide';document.querySelector('#menuButton').onclick=()=>document.querySelector('#topNav').classList.toggle('open');window.addEventListener('hashchange',()=>setPageFromHash(true));setPageFromHash(false)}
let routeTimer=0;function chapterMarkup(i){const c=chapters[i];if(!c)return;document.querySelector('#chapterContent').innerHTML=`<article class="chapter-content"><p class="eyebrow">${esc(c.group)} · FIELD GUIDE</p><h2>${esc(c.title)}</h2><p class="dek">${esc(c.intro)}</p>${c.sections.map(([h,p])=>`<h3>${esc(h)}</h3><p>${esc(p)}</p>`).join('')}<div class="callout">${esc(c.callout)}</div></article>`}
function setPageFromHash(animate){let route=decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1))||'home';const aliases={chapters:'guide',timeline:'history'};route=aliases[route]||route;const chapter=route.match(/^chapter\/(\d+)$/);const ids={home:'page-home',guide:'chapters',dragons:'dragons',history:'timeline',sources:'sources'};const targetId=chapter?'chapterView':ids[route]||'page-home';if(chapter)chapterMarkup(+chapter[1]);const loader=document.querySelector('#pageLoader'),label=document.querySelector('#loaderLabel'),titles={home:'RETURNING TO PYRRHIA',guide:'OPENING THE FIELD GUIDE',dragons:'ASSEMBLING THE DRAGON INDEX',history:'UNROLLING THE HISTORY',sources:'OPENING THE LIBRARY'};label.textContent=chapter?'OPENING '+(chapters[+chapter[1]]?.title||'CHAPTER').toUpperCase():titles[route]||titles.home;const reveal=()=>{document.querySelectorAll('.page').forEach(p=>p.classList.toggle('is-active',p.id===targetId));document.querySelectorAll('#topNav a').forEach(a=>a.classList.toggle('current',a.hash==='#'+route));window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'instant'});loader.classList.remove('is-visible');loader.setAttribute('aria-hidden','true');document.querySelector('#topNav').classList.remove('open')};clearTimeout(routeTimer);if(animate){loader.classList.add('is-visible');loader.setAttribute('aria-hidden','false');routeTimer=setTimeout(reveal,460)}else reveal()}
function showChapter(i){location.hash=`chapter/${i}`}
let dragonPage=0;const dragonPageSize=24;function sectionText(text,label){if(!text)return'';const match=text.match(new RegExp('== '+label+' ==([\\s\\S]*?)(?=\\n== |$)'));return match?match[1].replace(/\s+/g,' ').trim():''}
function renderDragons(){
 const q=document.querySelector('#search').value.trim().toLowerCase(),tribe=document.querySelector('#tribeFilter').value,curated=new Map(dragons.map(d=>[d[0],d])),catalog=window.dragonCatalog||[];
 const found=catalog.filter(entry=>{const d=curated.get(entry.name),t=d?d[1]:tribeOf(entry),hay=[entry.name,t,...(entry.categories||[]),entry.summary||'',...(d||[])].join(' ').toLowerCase();return(tribe==='all'||t===tribe||t.split(' / ').includes(tribe))&&hay.includes(q)});
 const pages=Math.max(1,Math.ceil(found.length/dragonPageSize));dragonPage=Math.min(dragonPage,pages-1);const shown=found.slice(dragonPage*dragonPageSize,(dragonPage+1)*dragonPageSize);document.querySelector('#resultCount').textContent=`${found.length} OF ${catalog.length} DRAGONS`;
 document.querySelector('#dragonGrid').innerHTML=shown.length?shown.map(entry=>{const d=curated.get(entry.name),tribeName=d?d[1]:tribeOf(entry),intro=(entry.summary||'').split(/\n\s*== /)[0].trim(),looks=sectionText(entry.summary,'Description'),personality=sectionText(entry.summary,'Personality'),bio=sectionText(entry.summary,'Biography');return d?`<article class="dragon-card"><div class="dragon-head"><h3>${esc(d[0])}</h3><span class="pill">${esc(d[1])}</span></div><p class="role">${esc(d[2])}</p><p><strong>Looks</strong><br>${esc(d[3])}</p><p><strong>Personality</strong><br>${esc(d[4])}</p><p><strong>Story role</strong><br>${esc(d[5])}</p><a class="wiki-link" href="${esc(entry.url)}" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Source article ↗</a></article>`:`<article class="dragon-card"><div class="dragon-head"><h3>${esc(entry.name)}</h3><span class="pill">${esc(tribeName)}</span></div><p class="role">${esc(intro||'Named dragon in the Wings of Fire community character catalog.')}</p>${looks?`<p><strong>Looks</strong><br>${esc(looks)}</p>`:'<p><strong>Looks</strong><br>See the linked character article; the catalog excerpt has no separate appearance section.</p>'}${personality?`<p><strong>Personality</strong><br>${esc(personality)}</p>`:'<p><strong>Personality</strong><br>See the linked character article; short appearances may not establish a detailed personality.</p>'}${bio?`<p><strong>Story role</strong><br>${esc(bio)}</p>`:`<p><strong>Story role</strong><br>${esc(intro||'See the linked article for appearances and role.')}</p>`}<a class="wiki-link" href="${esc(entry.url)}" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Full article & contributors ↗</a></article>`}).join(''):'<p>No dragons match that search. Try a name, tribe, or story role.</p>';
 const pagination=document.querySelector('#dragonPagination');pagination.innerHTML=pages>1?`<button class="page-button" ${dragonPage===0?'disabled':''} data-page="${dragonPage-1}">← Previous</button><span>Page ${dragonPage+1} of ${pages}</span><button class="page-button" ${dragonPage===pages-1?'disabled':''} data-page="${dragonPage+1}">Next →</button>`:'';pagination.querySelectorAll('[data-page]').forEach(button=>button.addEventListener('click',()=>{dragonPage=Number(button.dataset.page);renderDragons();document.querySelector('#dragons').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})}));
}
init();

